WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.200
"How to Suppress Your Appetite
and Naturally Boost GLP-1"

00:00:07.200 --> 00:00:08.240
About 20 years ago,

00:00:08.240 --> 00:00:10.080
there was a discovery
that would forever change

00:00:10.080 --> 00:00:12.800
our ideas about fiber:

00:00:12.800 --> 00:00:15.280
Our gut bacteria eat
the fiber we eat

00:00:15.280 --> 00:00:17.200
to produce important
signaling compounds

00:00:17.200 --> 00:00:19.760
called short-chain fatty acids.

00:00:19.760 --> 00:00:21.360
But before I get carried away,

00:00:21.360 --> 00:00:25.280
a little science lesson
to frame the discovery.

00:00:25.280 --> 00:00:27.520
Cells are the fundamental unit
of life.

00:00:27.520 --> 00:00:29.600
We’re composed
of trillions of them.

00:00:29.600 --> 00:00:30.880
They communicate with each other

00:00:30.880 --> 00:00:33.360
through receptors
on the surface of our cells.

00:00:33.360 --> 00:00:37.280
That’s how many hormones work;
it’s like a lock and key.

00:00:37.280 --> 00:00:39.280
Hormones are
signaling messengers,

00:00:39.280 --> 00:00:41.360
each with a unique shape.

00:00:41.360 --> 00:00:42.960
When released
into the bloodstream,

00:00:42.960 --> 00:00:44.080
they circulate
through the body

00:00:44.080 --> 00:00:46.560
until they find a receptor
they can fit into.

00:00:46.560 --> 00:00:48.560
Once the key is in the lock,

00:00:48.560 --> 00:00:49.760
it can turn on or off

00:00:49.760 --> 00:00:52.960
a whole series of reactions
in the target cell.

00:00:53.920 --> 00:00:55.520
The largest family
of cell receptors

00:00:55.520 --> 00:01:00.080
is known as G protein-coupled
receptors (GPCRs, for short).

00:01:00.080 --> 00:01:02.240
More than a third of the drugs
currently on the market

00:01:02.240 --> 00:01:04.720
work by plugging
into these receptors.

00:01:04.720 --> 00:01:07.440
That’s how drugs
like antihistamines work.

00:01:07.440 --> 00:01:09.760
We’ve discovered hundreds
of different GPCRs,

00:01:09.760 --> 00:01:13.600
but, remarkably, we don’t yet
know what many of them do.

00:01:13.600 --> 00:01:17.840
We have the lock; we just
don’t know what key fits.

00:01:17.840 --> 00:01:20.720
Accordingly, they’re called
"orphan" receptors.

00:01:20.720 --> 00:01:26.480
Two of these mystery receptors,
known only as GPR43 and GPR41,

00:01:26.480 --> 00:01:28.480
were found heavily expressed
throughout the body

00:01:28.480 --> 00:01:30.080
in our immune and fat cells,

00:01:30.080 --> 00:01:32.720
and in our gut, muscles,
and heart.

00:01:32.720 --> 00:01:34.320
We knew they must be vital,

00:01:34.320 --> 00:01:37.520
but we didn’t know what
activated them until 2003,

00:01:37.520 --> 00:01:39.520
when they were "deorphanized"

00:01:39.520 --> 00:01:42.320
(that’s actually
what scientists call it).

00:01:42.320 --> 00:01:44.560
The keys that fit
into those important locks

00:01:44.560 --> 00:01:48.000
were the short-chain fatty acids
that our gut bacteria make

00:01:48.000 --> 00:01:49.920
when we feed them fiber.

00:01:51.120 --> 00:01:54.320
This is how our gut bacteria
may communicate with us.

00:01:54.320 --> 00:01:56.240
Renamed
free fatty acid receptors,

00:01:56.240 --> 00:01:58.320
their existence is now
considered crucial insight

00:01:58.320 --> 00:02:00.640
into how fiber could play
such an important role

00:02:00.640 --> 00:02:03.600
in so many
of our chronic diseases.

00:02:03.600 --> 00:02:05.760
Hormones are defined
as signaling messengers

00:02:05.760 --> 00:02:06.960
produced in one organ

00:02:06.960 --> 00:02:08.480
that circulate
through the bloodstream

00:02:08.480 --> 00:02:11.760
and have a regulatory effect
on another organ.

00:02:11.760 --> 00:02:13.520
So, these
short-chain fatty acids

00:02:13.520 --> 00:02:15.760
can be considered hormones.

00:02:15.760 --> 00:02:19.440
It’s just that the organ that
produces them is our microbiome,

00:02:19.440 --> 00:02:22.160
the bacteria
that populate our gut.

00:02:22.160 --> 00:02:26.240
They can’t make these hormones,
though, without fiber.

00:02:26.240 --> 00:02:30.160
What do the free fatty acid
receptors on our fat cells do?

00:02:30.160 --> 00:02:32.960
Remember leptin, the hormone
produced by fat cells

00:02:32.960 --> 00:02:36.320
that helps tell our brain
how to regulate our body weight?

00:02:36.320 --> 00:02:37.440
Short-chain fatty acids

00:02:37.440 --> 00:02:39.600
stimulate the production
of leptin.

00:02:39.600 --> 00:02:40.480
This can be demonstrated

00:02:40.480 --> 00:02:43.120
in a petri dish
with individual fat cells,

00:02:43.120 --> 00:02:46.720
or by using fat tissue samples
removed during surgery.

00:02:46.720 --> 00:02:48.400
Drip on some
short-chain fatty acids

00:02:48.400 --> 00:02:52.560
and leptin production
almost doubles.

00:02:52.560 --> 00:02:54.800
Leptin is
an "anorectic" hormone,

00:02:54.800 --> 00:02:57.920
so-called because it generates
a loss of appetite and weight,

00:02:57.920 --> 00:03:00.240
but it does so
over the long term.

00:03:00.240 --> 00:03:01.840
Leptin levels slowly rise

00:03:01.840 --> 00:03:04.880
as the volume of body fat
gradually increases.

00:03:04.880 --> 00:03:06.480
There are other
anorectic hormones

00:03:06.480 --> 00:03:08.240
that instead work rapidly,

00:03:08.240 --> 00:03:11.920
signaling our brain
on more of a meal-to-meal basis.

00:03:11.920 --> 00:03:16.240
Two of these short-term appetite
suppressants are PYY and GLP-1,

00:03:16.240 --> 00:03:18.720
which are both secreted
by specialized L cells

00:03:18.720 --> 00:03:20.960
that line our colon.

00:03:20.960 --> 00:03:24.160
Guess what receptors are crowded
all over their surface?

00:03:24.160 --> 00:03:26.640
Free fatty acid receptors.

00:03:26.640 --> 00:03:28.880
Drip some short-chain
fatty acids onto L cells

00:03:28.880 --> 00:03:32.320
and they start churning out
PYY and GLP-1.

00:03:32.320 --> 00:03:35.120
You can do this in a petri dish
or in a person,

00:03:35.120 --> 00:03:36.480
either by infusing their rectums

00:03:36.480 --> 00:03:38.880
with a short-chain
fatty acid enema

00:03:38.880 --> 00:03:42.240
or the old-fashioned way,
by feeding people fiber,

00:03:42.240 --> 00:03:44.400
or even better,
fiber-rich foods,

00:03:44.400 --> 00:03:47.600
like this whole rye kernel
wheat bread.

00:03:47.600 --> 00:03:50.000
These hormones then get released
into the bloodstream,

00:03:50.000 --> 00:03:52.640
where they can shoot up into
the appetite center of our brain

00:03:52.640 --> 00:03:55.280
and turn down our cravings.

00:03:55.280 --> 00:03:59.440
The flipside is ghrelin,
the so-called hunger hormone.

00:03:59.440 --> 00:04:01.840
Ghrelin levels rise in our blood
before a meal

00:04:01.840 --> 00:04:03.200
to stimulate our appetite,

00:04:03.200 --> 00:04:05.760
and fall down again once we eat,

00:04:05.760 --> 00:04:09.840
before slowly building back up
to drive us back to the kitchen.

00:04:09.840 --> 00:04:12.880
But four hours after eating
just 24 grams of fiber,

00:04:12.880 --> 00:04:14.800
people’s ghrelin levels
are suppressed as much

00:04:14.800 --> 00:04:18.960
as if they had just had
500 calories’ worth of food.

00:04:18.960 --> 00:04:21.200
Longer studies show
similar effects.

00:04:21.200 --> 00:04:23.920
11- and 12-year-old children
with overweight or obesity

00:04:23.920 --> 00:04:28.000
who were randomized to increased
fiber intake for 16 weeks

00:04:28.000 --> 00:04:30.240
ended up eating
hundreds of fewer calories

00:04:30.240 --> 00:04:31.600
of a buffet meal,

00:04:31.600 --> 00:04:34.312
compared to the placebo
control group.

